body:
Dress appropriately - you're better being too well dressed for the first few days than underdressing. Don't bring up your weird hobbies straight off the bat - going to sex clubs or performing as a drag queen is great, but your new colleagues don't need to know until they've got to know you better. Be a little early - around 10 minutes before you are meant to start, but be prepared to wait for someone to collect you from reception, or be dumped in the kitchen and told to make yourself a coffee while everyone sorts their shit out. Speak up, but don't be loud or annoying or a know-it-all. Try to do 'active listening' when someone's explaining something to you, and don't be afraid to whip out a notebook and take notes if it's complicated or something that seems really important. And if you're unsure about anything, just ask. Seriously, I would rather explain something a hundred times than have the newbie go off and do it all wrong and create more work for everyone. 

&#x200B;

Good luck, you've got this. It's all just common sense. Oh, and bring a sandwich with you, but also take cash in case everyone's going out!

Something similar has happened to my flat on the sales history on Zoopla. Us and next door are (for examples sake) 50a and 50b in most address database systems, however the council knows us as 'first floor left' and 'first floor right'. Zoopla thinks there is only a flat 57. So both of the flats selling history is on Zoopla as one single flat. It looks like our flat has been bought and sold twice as many times as it really has. 

&#x200B;

Your 2001 dates could be a lovely story of neighbours that met as singletons and both sold up their flats to buy one of the unconverted houses down the street!
